module bindings.php.expression_values_live_service_exposure;

// Symfony's ExpressionLanguage — and every evaluator that shares its shape —
// runs the expression text over a values map the caller supplies, and the
// component's documentation is explicit that the expression may call any
// public method on every object in that map: the values map is the
// evaluator's security boundary. Sulu's route generator passed its shared
// translator service straight through, so a route schema expression could
// call `translator.addResource('php', $path)` — the translator's documented
// resource registration, whose `php` loader includes the file — and turn a
// URL pattern into arbitrary file inclusion. The remediation passes a
// purpose-built facade whose only members are the calls the expression
// needs; the same hardening is the advisory's own workaround for users who
// cannot upgrade.
//
// The binding states the API fact rather than the incident: an evaluator
// call whose values argument is an array literal binding an entry to a
// property of the enclosing object — `['name' => $this->service]` — exposes
// that service's whole public API to expression text. A value computed for
// the evaluation does not carry the `=>$this->` spelling, because the
// property sits inside the expression that computes the value (a facade
// constructor, a factory call) rather than directly after the `=>`; a local
// variable carries no `$this->` at all. That is the same incomplete template
// validation already reported when a renderer runs untrusted templates with
// no sandbox at all.
//
// `node.context.call == "evaluate"` is exact-token and function-confined in
// PHP class methods, and it is what keeps the flag off sibling methods of a
// class whose one method builds the exposure; the `callArg` needles then
// read the compacted argument text of that function's calls. Both the
// bracketed and the legacy `array(...)` spellings of the values map are
// matched. Residuals, both directions stated rather than hidden: the
// `=>$this->` witness may come from any call argument of the same function,
// so a function that evaluates over a values map while also passing a
// service property inside another call's array is reported once for the
// pair; and a values map built into a variable or merged from another array
// (`array_merge($context, [...])`) carries no `=>$this->` of its own and is
// not reported, while a facade reached through a factory call
// (`['t' => $this->buildFacade()]`) still spells `=>$this->` and is — the
// value is computed from the service, and the review is where that settles.
binding expressionValuesLiveServiceExposure {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.scope == "function"
    and node.context.language == "php"
    and node.context.call == "evaluate"
    and containsAny(node.context.callArg, ["evaluate:['", "evaluate:array("])
    and node.context.callArg contains "=>$this->"
  emit issue code.IncompleteTemplateValidation at node
}
